ntors><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>INOUE AKIHISA</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>NISHIYAMA NOBUYUKI</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="epo"><addressbook><name>ZHANG TAU</name><address><country>JP</country></address></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>INOUE, AKIHISA</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>NISHIYAMA, NOBUYUKI</name></addressbook></inventor><inventor status="new" format="intermediate"><addressbook><name>ZHANG, TAU</name></addressbook></inventor></inventors></parties></bibliographic-data><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="EN"><p>A molten alloy capable of forming an amorphous alloy is subjected to solidification under a pressure of more than 1 atm. to form an alloy having no defect, and also the cooling rate during the solidification is controlled so as to disperse fine crystals having a mean crystal grain diameter of 1 nm to 50  mu m in a crystal volume percentage of 5 to 40 %, to thereby impart a uniform residual compression stress over the whole resultant amorphous alloy ingot. Further, an amorphous alloy ingot produced by this method can be strengthened by heating the alloy ingot at a specific temperature rising rate and rendering, in a supercooled liquid state before crystallization, at least one of boron, carbon, oxygen, nitrogen and fluorine to penetrate from its surface, to thereby precipitate a high melting point compound thereof with an element forming the amorphous alloy within the alloy ingot.</p></abstract><abstract load-source="docdb" source="national office" status="new" lang="FR"><p>Un alliage fondu capable de former un alliage amorphe est soumis à une solidification sous une pression supérieure à 1 atm afin de former un alliage ne présentant aucun défaut, et la vitesse de refroidissement pendant la solidification est régulée afin de disperser des cristaux fins ayant un diamètre moyen de grain de cristaux compris entre 1 nm et 50  mu m dans un pourcentage volumique de cristaux compris entre 5 et 40 %, afin de conférer ainsi une contrainte de compression résiduelle uniforme sur tout le lingot d'alliage amorphe obtenu. Ensuite, on peut renforcer un lingot d'alliage amorphe produit selon ce procédé en chauffant le lingot d'alliage à une vitesse de montée de température spécifique et en faisant pénétrer, dans un état liquide superrefroidi avant cristallisation, du bore et/ou du carbone et/ou de l'oxygène et/ou de l'azote et/ou du fluor, à partir de sa surface, afin de précipiter ainsi un composé de celui-ci à point de fusion élevé avec un élément formant l'alliage amorphe dans le lingot d'alliage.</p></abstract><copyright>User acknowledges that the Information Retrieval Facility (IRF) and its third party providers retain all right, title and interest in and to this xml under applicable copyright laws. User acquires no ownership rights to this xml including but not limited to its format. User hereby accepts the terms and conditions of the Licence Agreement set forth at http://www.ir-facility.org/legal/marec/data_licence</copyright></patent-document>